SOLUTIONS = {"In Text 1, why was Janet home from school on Valentine's Day?": "Step 1: Look for the sentence that explains why Janet stayed home.\n\nStep 2: The text says Janet 'stayed home from school because she was sick.'\n\nStep 3: This directly answers the question — rule out the other options since the passage never mentions a dentist appointment, disliking parties, or school being cancelled.\n\nAnswer: C (She was sick).", "In Text 1, what did Marcela do when she got home from the Valentine's party?": "Step 1: Find what Marcela did right after the party.\n\nStep 2: The text says 'Marcela walked right past her sister. She had no plan to give her any of the candy.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option B exactly, and rules out A (she didn't share yet) and C/D (not mentioned).\n\nAnswer: B.", 'In Text 1, who noticed that Marcela was being rude to Janet?': "Step 1: Find who reacts to Marcela's behavior.\n\nStep 2: The text says 'Just then, their mother walked into the kitchen. She noticed that Marcela was being rude.'\n\nStep 3: The mother is the one who notices — not Janet, a teacher, or a neighbor.\n\nAnswer: C (Their mother).", "In Text 2, why wouldn't Jason pass the ball to his teammates?": "Step 1: Find Jason's reason for not passing.\n\nStep 2: The text says 'He was so close to scoring a goal, and he loved the feeling when he scored.'\n\nStep 3: This shows he wanted the good feeling of scoring himself, not that he couldn't see teammates or was told not to pass.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'What did Coach Terry tell Jason at halftime in Text 2?': "Step 1: Find Coach Terry's exact words at halftime.\n\nStep 2: He said, 'Soccer is a team sport... If you can't be a team player, you can't be on this team.'\n\nStep 3: This is a message about teamwork and passing, not praise for scoring (A) or a request to switch positions (C).\n\nAnswer: B.", 'How are the main characters in Text 1 (Marcela) and Text 2 (Jason) MOST similar?': 'Step 1: Think about how Marcela and Jason each acted at the start of their stories.\n\nStep 2: Marcela kept her candy to herself, and Jason kept the ball to himself — both were being selfish.\n\nStep 3: Both are then taught a lesson (Marcela by her mother, Jason by Coach Terry) and change their behavior.\n\nAnswer: C (Both act selfishly and learn a lesson).', 'By the end of Text 2, how does Jason decide to change his behavior?': "Step 1: Find what Jason decides at the end of the story.\n\nStep 2: The text says 'he would pass the ball to his teammates from now on. It was time to give them a chance...'\n\nStep 3: This matches passing the ball to give teammates a chance, not quitting or scoring more alone.\n\nAnswer: B.", "Read this passage and draw a conclusion:\n'Anxious faces stared at the clock. The groom fiddled with his thumbs and combed his hair nervously. Finally, the violinist began a sweet-sounding melody and everyone rose in their seats.'\n\nWhere are these people most likely?": "Step 1: Look for clues in the passage: 'groom,' a violinist playing, and everyone rising.\n\nStep 2: A groom and people standing for music are classic signs of a wedding ceremony.\n\nStep 3: There's no clue pointing to a concert hall, restaurant, or birthday party.\n\nAnswer: D (A wedding).", "Read this passage and draw a conclusion:\n'My bags were ready. The only thing left to pack was the food. My parents were coming too. They always brought enough food to feed an army. I called Tempest to the car. She wagged her tail happily — ready for her first overnight adventure in nature.'\n\nWhere is the family going?": "Step 1: Look for clues: 'overnight adventure in nature,' lots of food, and the dog coming along.\n\nStep 2: These details point to a trip spent outdoors overnight — camping or hiking.\n\nStep 3: A hotel, party, or mall wouldn't need an 'overnight adventure in nature.'\n\nAnswer: C (A camping or hiking trip).", "Read this passage and draw a conclusion:\n'Mia wiped sweat from her brow and looked down at the script one last time. She had rehearsed every waking moment. She walked on stage left, determined to show them just how hard she had worked.'\n\nWhat did Mia rehearse?": "Step 1: Look for clues: 'script,' 'stage,' and walking on 'stage left.'\n\nStep 2: These are all specific to theater performances.\n\nStep 3: A speech, gymnastics routine, or musical instrument wouldn't involve a script and a stage.\n\nAnswer: B (A play or theater performance).", "Read this passage and draw a conclusion:\n'The buses raced around the neighborhood making an unfamiliar sound after the long, blistering hot summer. Kids with shiny new backpacks put smiles on parents' faces. The nervous chatter at the bus stop eased some of the tension.'\n\nWhat time of year is it most likely?": "Step 1: Look for clues: 'after the long, blistering hot summer,' new backpacks, buses, and nervous kids.\n\nStep 2: These details describe the first day back to school in the fall.\n\nStep 3: Winter break, spring break, and summer vacation don't fit with new backpacks and buses returning.\n\nAnswer: C (The start of a new school year in fall).", "Read this passage and draw a conclusion:\n'Santiago was going to have a blast, even if he didn't know anyone. He stood at the front door and took a deep breath. Earlier that morning he'd wrapped his gift in superhero wrapping paper and was sure his cousin would love it. He rang the bell.'\n\nWhy did Santiago have a present?": "Step 1: Look for clues: a wrapped gift, ringing a doorbell, and excitement about having fun.\n\nStep 2: Bringing a wrapped gift to someone's door points to attending their birthday party.\n\nStep 3: It's not his own birthday (he brought the gift for his cousin), and there's no mention of delivering a package for work.\n\nAnswer: B (He was going to a birthday party).", 'Read the passage about Elias. What did Elias do INSTEAD of starting his social studies project?\n(Passage: Elias had a big project due. He had two weeks but put it off — he went skateboarding, played video games, and even cleaned his room to avoid it.)': "Step 1: Find what Elias did instead of working.\n\nStep 2: The passage says he 'went skateboarding with his friends at the park, and he played video games with his brother.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option B exactly.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'How did Elias feel when he turned in his finished project the next day?': "Step 1: Find how Elias felt about his finished project.\n\nStep 2: The passage says 'Elias was not proud of the work he had completed. He believed it would have been much better if he spent more time on it.'\n\nStep 3: This rules out A and B (he wasn't proud or happy) — he felt his rushed work fell short.\n\nAnswer: C.", 'What is the MAIN lesson of the story about Elias?': "Step 1: Think about what Elias learned from procrastinating.\n\nStep 2: He wishes he had 'been more responsible' and started earlier, since rushing hurt the quality of his work.\n\nStep 3: The lesson is about starting early and being responsible, not that projects are boring or friends matter more than school.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'Which strategy does the Short and Sweet Summaries worksheet teach you to use when writing a fiction summary?': "Step 1: Recall the strategy the worksheet names for summarizing fiction.\n\nStep 2: It says: 'Use the writing strategy of Somebody Wanted But So Then to help you write a short fiction summary!'\n\nStep 3: This matches option B directly.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'According to Text 1, in what year did Harriet Tubman escape slavery and reach Pennsylvania?': "Step 1: Find the sentence about her escape.\n\nStep 2: Text 1 says: 'Harriet Tubman was born a slave in Maryland around 1820. In 1849, she escaped to Pennsylvania.'\n\nStep 3: The escape year is 1849, not her birth year (1820).\n\nAnswer: B (1849).", 'What was the Underground Railroad?': "Step 1: Find the definition given in the text.\n\nStep 2: Text 1 says: 'The Underground Railroad was a network, or group, of people who worked together to bring slaves to freedom... a secret route, or path.'\n\nStep 3: It was not a real train (A) — the text clarifies it was a network of people and routes.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'According to Text 1, how many South Carolina slaves did Harriet Tubman help free in 1863?': "Step 1: Find the sentence about the 1863 raid.\n\nStep 2: Text 1 says: 'In 1863, she led a raid that freed 750 South Carolina slaves.'\n\nStep 3: The number is 750, matching option C.\n\nAnswer: C (750).", "What does the word 'conductor' mean in the context of the Underground Railroad?": "Step 1: Find how the text defines 'conductor.'\n\nStep 2: Text 1 says: 'The people who guided the slaves from place to place were called conductors.'\n\nStep 3: This is a special use of the word, not related to trains or music in this context.\n\nAnswer: C.", 'According to Text 2, why was running away from slavery so dangerous in the 1800s?': "Step 1: Find the reason given in Text 2.\n\nStep 2: It says: 'If a runaway slave was found, their owners would often hurt them and punish them.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option C — the danger was punishment from owners, not weather or lack of safe houses.\n\nAnswer: C.", 'What three roles did Harriet Tubman serve in the Union Army according to Text 2?': "Step 1: Find the three roles listed in Text 2.\n\nStep 2: It says: 'she worked for the Union Army as a cook, a nurse, and even a spy.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option B (cook, nurse, spy) exactly.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'What topic do BOTH Text 1 and Text 2 have in common?': 'Step 1: Think about what both Text 1 and Text 2 describe.\n\nStep 2: Both texts focus on Harriet Tubman helping enslaved people escape and her connection to the Underground Railroad.\n\nStep 3: The other options only appear in one text or not at all.\n\nAnswer: C.', 'According to Text 2, where did Harriet Tubman go after escaping slavery to find work?': "Step 1: Find where Harriet went after escaping, according to Text 2.\n\nStep 2: It says: 'She made it all the way to Philadelphia, where she was able to find work.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option C, not New York, Washington D.C., or Boston.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Read this passage:\n'The West Indian manatee has been listed as an endangered species... Federal and state laws protect the manatee. Scientists are working to find ways to help the manatee survive.'\n\nWhat is the author's viewpoint about manatees?": "Step 1: Look at how the author talks about manatees — mentioning laws that protect them and scientists working to help them survive.\n\nStep 2: This shows the author believes manatees are endangered and worth protecting.\n\nStep 3: There's no support for the idea that there are too many manatees or that they're dangerous.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'Which sentence from the manatee passage BEST shows the author believes manatees need help?': "Step 1: Look for the sentence that shows active effort to help manatees.\n\nStep 2: 'Federal and state laws protect the manatee. Scientists are working to find ways to help the manatee survive' directly shows support for manatees.\n\nStep 3: The other sentences are neutral facts, not statements of the author's belief.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Read this passage:\n'Did you know how bad soda is for your health? Soda has a lot of calories and too much sugar. Soda has been linked to diabetes and obesity. Stick to water for a healthier life!'\n\nWhat is the author's viewpoint about soda?": "Step 1: Notice the negative facts the author lists about soda (calories, sugar, diabetes, obesity).\n\nStep 2: The passage ends with 'Stick to water for a healthier life!' — a direct recommendation against soda.\n\nStep 3: This shows the author's viewpoint is that soda is unhealthy.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Which sentence from the soda passage MOST DIRECTLY shows the author's viewpoint?": "Step 1: Look for the sentence that states an opinion or recommendation, not just a fact.\n\nStep 2: 'Stick to water for a healthier life!' is a direct piece of advice — the clearest sign of the author's viewpoint.\n\nStep 3: The other options are questions or facts, not the author's direct opinion.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Read this passage:\n'Recess helps kids focus and stay on-task better when they are in the classroom. Going outside for recess is good for your health... Kids get to run around, which is good for physical health. Recess is a great time for kids to play together and build relationships.'\n\nWhat is the author's viewpoint about recess?": "Step 1: Notice all the benefits of recess listed: better focus, physical health, and building relationships.\n\nStep 2: Listing several benefits shows the author believes recess is good for kids.\n\nStep 3: There's no support in the passage for reducing recess or that it only helps kids who like sports.\n\nAnswer: C.", "'Point of view' or 'viewpoint' is best described as:": "Step 1: Recall how the worksheet defines viewpoint.\n\nStep 2: It says: 'Point of view or viewpoint is the way we feel about the topic or events in a piece of text.'\n\nStep 3: This matches option B, not the setting, character name, or story beginning.\n\nAnswer: B.", 'Choose the correct word:\n\'"_______ up first," I told the team. Everyone ran out to _______ places on the field.\'': "Step 1: The first blank needs a word meaning 'they are' — that's 'they're.'\n\nStep 2: The second blank needs a possessive word — 'their places' means the places belonging to them.\n\nStep 3: 'They're up first... their places' matches option A.\n\nAnswer: A.", "Choose the correct word:\n'The air was perfectly still. _______ wasn't the slightest breeze.'": "Step 1: This sentence needs a word that shows something exists (or doesn't) — 'there wasn't a breeze.'\n\nStep 2: 'There' is used this way as an adverb, not 'their' (possessive) or 'they're' (they are).\n\nAnswer: C (There).", "Choose the correct word:\n'_______ were still three more balls to go.'": "Step 1: 'There were three more balls' uses 'there' to show something existed.\n\nStep 2: This is not possession ('their') or a contraction of 'they are' ('they're').\n\nAnswer: C (There).", 'Choose the correct word:\n\'From the stands I heard chanting: "_______ gonna lose! We\'re gonna win!"\'': "Step 1: 'They're gonna lose' means 'they are going to lose.'\n\nStep 2: Only 'they're' expands correctly to 'they are.'\n\nAnswer: C (They're).", "Choose the correct word:\n'I looked at the opposite team leaning against the fence. _______ faces were tense with determination.'": "Step 1: 'Their faces' means the faces belonging to the opposing team — that's possession.\n\nStep 2: Possessive words like this use 'their,' with no apostrophe.\n\nAnswer: C (Their).", "Choose the correct word:\n'I turned to my team, and saw smiles on _______ faces.'": "Step 1: 'Their faces' shows the faces belong to the teammates — that's possessive.\n\nStep 2: Use 'their' (no apostrophe) for possession.\n\nAnswer: C (their).", "Which sentence uses 'there' correctly?": "Step 1: 'There' should be used to show location or existence, like 'over there.'\n\nStep 2: 'Put the books over there on the shelf' uses 'there' correctly as a location word.\n\nStep 3: The other options use 'there' where 'they're' or 'their' belongs.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Which sentence uses 'their' correctly?": "Step 1: 'Their' should show something belongs to a group.\n\nStep 2: 'The students forgot their homework again' — the homework belongs to the students.\n\nStep 3: The other options misuse 'their' where 'they're' or 'there' belongs.\n\nAnswer: C.", "Which sentence uses 'they're' correctly?": "Step 1: 'They're' should expand to 'they are.'\n\nStep 2: 'They're going to the movies tonight' = 'They are going to the movies tonight' — this works.\n\nStep 3: The other options misuse 'they're' where 'their' or 'there' belongs.\n\nAnswer: C.", "'Their' is a _______ pronoun — it shows something belongs to a group of people.": "Step 1: 'Their' shows something belongs to a group of people, just like 'his' or 'her' shows individual ownership.\n\nAnswer: C (possessive).", "'They're' is a _______ — a shortened form of two words joined together.": "Step 1: 'They're' is a shortened form of two words joined together: 'they' + 'are.'\n\nStep 2: The apostrophe replaces the missing letter 'a.'\n\nAnswer: D (contraction).", "Choose the correct word:\n'The tiger licked _______ paw.'": "Step 1: The paw belongs to the tiger — that's possession.\n\nStep 2: Possessive 'its' never has an apostrophe.\n\nAnswer: A (its).", "Choose the correct word:\n'I wonder if _______ going to rain tomorrow.'": "Step 1: 'I wonder if it is going to rain' — the sentence needs the contraction of 'it is.'\n\nStep 2: 'It's' (with an apostrophe) means 'it is.'\n\nAnswer: B (it's).", "Choose the correct word:\n'_______ time to go to school.'": "Step 1: 'It's time' means 'it is time.'\n\nStep 2: Use the contraction 'it's' with an apostrophe.\n\nAnswer: B (It's).", "Choose the correct word:\n'My cat and _______ kittens are taking a nap.'": "Step 1: The kittens belong to the cat — that's possession.\n\nStep 2: Use possessive 'its' with no apostrophe.\n\nAnswer: A (its).", "Choose the correct word:\n'_______ going to be a long walk to the train.'": "Step 1: 'It's going to be' means 'it is going to be.'\n\nStep 2: Use the contraction 'it's.'\n\nAnswer: B (It's).", "Choose the correct word:\n'Your puppy is so cute — what's _______ name?'": "Step 1: The name belongs to the puppy — that's possession.\n\nStep 2: Use possessive 'its' with no apostrophe.\n\nAnswer: A (its).", "'Its' (without an apostrophe) is a _______ pronoun — it shows something belongs to it.": "Step 1: 'Its' (without an apostrophe) shows ownership, just like 'his' or 'her.'\n\nAnswer: C (possessive).", "'It's' (with an apostrophe) is a _______ — a shortened form of 'it is.'": "Step 1: 'It's' (with an apostrophe) is a shortened form of 'it is.'\n\nStep 2: The apostrophe replaces the letter 'i' in 'is.'\n\nAnswer: B (contraction).", "'Have you seen my toy? _______ not in _______ box.'": "Step 1: The first blank means 'it is not' — use the contraction 'it's.'\n\nStep 2: The second blank shows the box belongs to the toy — use possessive 'its.'\n\nStep 3: 'It's not in its box' matches option A.\n\nAnswer: A.", "Which sentence uses 'it's' correctly?": "Step 1: 'It's' should expand to 'it is.'\n\nStep 2: 'It's a beautiful day outside' = 'It is a beautiful day outside' — this works.\n\nStep 3: The other options use 'it's' where possessive 'its' belongs (the dog's tail, the bird's nest, the cat's paw).\n\nAnswer: B."}

def fetch_grade4_questions(subject):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?grade=eq.4&subject=eq.{subject}&select=id,text&limit=500"
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"apikey": KEY, "Authorization": f"Bearer {KEY}"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())

def patch_solution(qid, solution):
    url = f"{BASE}/questions?id=eq.{qid}"
    data = json.dumps({"solution": solution}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=data, method="PATCH", headers=HEADERS)
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as r:
        return r.status

print("Fetching grade 4 Reading questions...")
questions = fetch_grade4_questions("Reading")
print(f"Found {len(questions)} questions")

matched = 0
skipped = 0
for q in questions:
    text = q["text"]
    solution = SOLUTIONS.get(text)
    if solution:
        status = patch_solution(q["id"], solution)
        print(f"  \u2713 [{status}] {text[:60]}...")
        matched += 1
        time.sleep(0.1)
    else:
        skipped += 1

print(f"\nDone: {matched} updated, {skipped} not in this script's list")
